  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nFrom Saturday onward, a slow, steady warming trend is expected to \ndevelop as an upper level ridge moves overhead, and south-\nsouthwesterly low level flow becomes re-established. Surface high \npressure and a dry-continental airmass should also keep \nprecipitation chances minimal. \n\nAfter a very chilly morning Saturday, temperatures are likely to \nrebound only into the low 60s, with a slight increase from the day \nbefore thanks largely to clear skies. Overnight, another very chilly \nnight is expected, as surface winds will remain very light and \nradiative cooling is expected to be quite efficient. Current \ndeterministic forecasts suggest that morning lows in the mid to \nupper 30s should be common area-wide, with another opportunity for a \nbrief freeze in Ozark river valleys in particular thanks to cool-air \ndrainage. Sunday morning should be the last opportunity for a \nfreeze, although some patchy frost will be in play again Monday \nmorning. From Sunday afternoon onward, southerly low level flow \nbecomes firmly established, and temperatures will steadily warm each \nday through mid-week. Temperatures are expected to climb back to \nnear average by Sunday afternoon, with high confidence (90+%) in \nabove average temperatures Tuesday onward, and a reasonable chance \nto exceed 80 degrees (40-60%) Wednesday onward. Meanwhile, little \nadditional precipitation is expected for the next week.",
